[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer**

*Use a **`color-relief`** layer.*  
It reads the same `raster-dem` source you already have and lets you paint the DEM directly with an
`interpolate` expression on the special `["elevation"]` value. The ramp you define in the
`color-relief-color` paint property is the hypsometric tint (blue → green → brown → white).

Below is a complete, ready-to-copy example that shows:

1. Adding the DEM source (`type: "raster-dem"`).  
2. Adding a `color-relief` layer that colors the terrain by elevation.  
3. Optional tweaks (opacity, resampling, exaggeration, sky) so you can see the effect in 3-D.

---

## 1. Add the DEM source (you probably already have this)

```js
// Assuming you already created the map instance:
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
  pitch: 60,               // tilt so you can see the 3-D effect
  bearing: 0
});

// DEM source – replace the URL with your own tilejson or tile URL template
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://mytileserver.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // or a TileJSON URL
  tileSize: 256,
  // optional: encoding if you use Terrarium or Mapbox (default is "mapbox")
  // encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12
});
```

> **Note** – The source is *only* a `raster-dem`. Do **not** add a `raster` layer for it; the
> `color-relief` layer will read the elevation values directly.

---

## 2. Add the **`color-relief`** layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hypsometric-tint',
  type: 'color-relief',          // <-- the layer type you need
  source: 'terrain-dem',         // the raster-dem source defined above
  paint: {
    // The ramp – an interpolate expression over the special ["elevation"] value.
    // Elevation is in **meters** above the DEM’s vertical datum.
    'color-relief-color': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['elevation'],

      // ---- elevation (m) ----   ---- colour ----
      -10,   '#2e3f5a',   // deep water (a dark blue)
       0,    '#2c7bb6',   // sea level – bright blue
     200,    '#61a6d1',   // shallow water / low coast
     500,    '#a6d96a',   // lowlands – green
    1500,    '#ffffbf',   // mid-elevation – light yellow
    2500,    '#fdae61',   // sub-alpine – orange / brown
    3500,    '#d73027',   // alpine – red-brown
    4500,    '#ffffff'    // highest peaks – white snow
    ],

    // Optional: make the layer semi-transparent so underlying imagery can be seen
    'color-relief-opacity': 0.85,

    // Optional: control how the DEM is sampled when the map is zoomed out.
    // Values: "linear", "nearest". Default is "linear".
    // 'resampling': 'linear'
  }
});
```

### How the ramp works

* `['interpolate', ['linear'], ['elevation'], …]` tells MapLibre to linearly interpolate between the
  elevation break-points you provide.
* Each pair **elevation-in-meters → CSS colour** defines a “band”.  
  You can add as many break-points as you like; the engine will smoothly blend between them.
* The expression is **only valid inside `color-relief-color`** – you cannot reuse `["elevation"]` in other
  layer types.

---

## 3. (Optional) Turn on 3-D terrain & sky so you can see the effect in perspective

```js
// Enable the mesh that lifts the DEM off the flat plane
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  exaggeration: 1.0   // increase >1.0 if you want more dramatic relief
});

// Add a sky so the horizon isn’t just the page background
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#cceeff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

> **Why the sky matters** – When the camera is pitched (`pitch > 0`) the area above the terrain is
> filled by the sky object. Without it you’ll see a blank (usually white) background that looks like
> “missing terrain”.

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre hypsometric tint with color-relief</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 60,
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // 2️⃣ Color-relief layer (hypsometric tint)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hypsometric-tint',
      type: 'color-relief',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'color-relief-color': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['elevation'],
          -10, '#2e3f5a',
           0,  '#2c7bb6',
         200,  '#61a6d1',
         500,  '#a6d96a',
        1500,  '#ffffbf',
        2500,  '#fdae61',
        3500,  '#d73027',
        4500,  '#ffffff'
        ],
        'color-relief-opacity': 0.85
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ Enable terrain mesh + sky (optional but nice)
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#cceeff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or paste the code into a CodePen/JSFiddle) and you’ll see the DEM rendered
with a smooth blue-green-brown-white gradient, all computed **client-side** – no pre-colored raster tiles
required.

---

## 5. Quick checklist

| ✅ | What you need |
|---|---|
| **Layer type** | `type: "color-relief"` |
| **Source** | A `raster-dem` source (the same one you use for `setTerrain` if you have 3-D). |
| **Paint property** | `color-relief-color` – an `interpolate` expression over `["elevation"]`. |
| **Optional paint** | `color-relief-opacity` (0-1), `resampling` (`"linear"` or `"nearest"`). |
| **No** | `raster-color`, `raster-value`, `raster-color-mix`, or any `raster` layer – those are **not** understood by MapLibre for DEMs. |
| **Version** | MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.6 (the `color-relief` layer was introduced in GL JS 5.6). |

That’s it! You now have a client-side hypsometric tint that updates instantly when you pan/zoom,
without any extra tile generation on the server. Happy mapping!

### Short answer  
Yes – you can draw contour lines **directly from a `raster-dem` source at runtime**.  
The trick is to let a small helper library (`maplibre-contour`) turn the DEM tiles into vector-contour tiles **in a Web-Worker**, register a custom protocol for those tiles, and then add a normal `line` layer that reads the generated vector source.

Below is a step-by-step recipe, plus notes on the most common gotchas.

---

## 1. What you need

| Piece | Why it’s required |
|------|-------------------|
| **A `raster-dem` source** | Holds the elevation data (Terrarium, Mapbox, or any other supported encoding). |
| **`maplibre-contour` library** | Reads the DEM tiles, runs a marching-squares / contour algorithm in a worker, and serves the result through a custom URL protocol (`contour://`). |
| **A vector source that points to the protocol URL** | MapLibre can only consume vector data, so the library pretends the contours are a normal vector tileset. |
| **A `line` (or `fill`) layer** | Renders the contour geometry. |
| **(Optional) a style for major/minor contours** | Different line-width / colour / label rules. |

All of this lives on the client – no pre-generated contour tileset, no server-side processing.

---

## 2. Install the helper

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour   # or: yarn add maplibre-contour
```

If you are not using a bundler you can also load the UMD build from a CDN:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-contour@latest/dist/maplibre-contour.umd.js"></script>
```

The global variable will be `maplibreContour` (or `mlcontour` in the examples below).

---

