Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns the blank “page-background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are native MapLibre GL JS – no extra plugins are needed.

---

## 1. Prerequisites – what you already have

```js
// 1️⃣ 3-D terrain (raster-dem source) – already on
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
  tileSize: 256,
  // encoding must match the DEM (e.g. "terrarium" or "mapbox")
  // encoding: 'terrarium',
});

map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

// 2️⃣  Camera pitched so you can see the side of the terrain
map.easeTo({ pitch: 70, bearing: 0, zoom: 12 });
```

If you can see the hills and valleys but the area **above the horizon is just the page’s background colour**, you are missing the **sky object** (and optionally fog) that MapLibre draws behind the terrain.

---

## 2. Add a *sky* object

### 2.1. The simplest “sky-only” call

```js
map.setSky({
  // The colour of the dome directly overhead
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',          // light blue

  // How quickly the colour blends into the horizon line
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,        // 0 = hard line, 1 = very gradual

  // Colour of the horizon itself (the line where sky meets ground)
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',     // white / pale

  // OPTIONAL – make the sky look a little atmospheric
  // (these three properties together create the “haze” effect)
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,        // blend horizon colour into fog
  'fog-color': '#cce6ff',          // a very light blue-grey fog
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5          // how far the fog reaches down the terrain
});
```

That’s it – the sky now appears behind the terrain whenever the map is pitched.

### 2.2. Where does `setSky` go?

*You can call it any time after the map is created* (e.g. right after `map.on('load', …)`).  
If you prefer to keep everything in the style JSON, you can also declare the sky object there:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { … },
  "terrain": { "source": "terrain-dem", "exaggeration": 1 },
  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#199EF3",
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.5,
    "fog-color": "#cce6ff",
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5
  },
  "layers": [ … ]
}
```

If you edit the style JSON, just reload the style (`map.setStyle(styleObject)`) or start the map with that style.

---

## 3. Why the *fog* (haze) works only with terrain

- **Fog lives inside the sky object** (`fog-color`, `horizon-fog-blend`, `fog-ground-blend`).  
- It **requires 3-D terrain** to have a surface for the fog to “cling” to.  
- If you call `map.setSky()` **before** `map.setTerrain()`, the fog will be ignored (MapLibre silently drops it).  
- The fog colour is blended **downwards** from the horizon; the larger `fog-ground-blend` is, the farther the haze reaches onto the terrain.

> **Tip:** Use a very light fog colour (e.g. `#cce6ff` or `rgba(200,220,255,0.6)`) so the terrain is still visible but looks atmospheric. Increase the blend values if you want a thicker haze.

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – sky + fog + terrain</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
          tileSize: 256,
          // encoding: 'terrarium'   // uncomment if needed
        },
        // add any raster or vector source you want to drape over the terrain
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'https://tiles.example.com/satellite/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      terrain: { source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 },
      sky: {
        'sky-color': '#199EF3',
        'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
        'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
        'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
        'fog-color': '#cce6ff',
        'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
      },
      layers: [
        // base imagery draped over the terrain
        { id: 'satellite', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true               // required for 3-D terrain
  });

  // If you prefer to set the sky after the map loads:
  // map.on('load', () => map.setSky({ …same object as above… }));
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Result:**  
*The map shows the terrain, the sky dome above the horizon, and a subtle blue-grey haze that fades into the ground.*

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to debug them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No sky at all (still blank background)** | `map.setSky()` called **before** `map.setTerrain()` or terrain not yet loaded. | Call `setSky` *after* `setTerrain`, or put the sky object in the style JSON (which is applied after terrain). |
| **Fog never appears** | `fog-color` omitted, or terrain disabled, or `sky-horizon-blend` set to `0`. | Add a non-transparent `fog-color` and make sure terrain is on. |
| **Sky looks too “hard” (sharp line at horizon)** | `sky-horizon-blend` (or `horizon-fog-blend`) is `0`. | Increase those values (0.3-0.7 works well). |
| **Sky colour looks washed out / too dark** | Using an opaque `fog-color` that covers the sky, or `sky-color` too close to `fog-color`. | Choose a sky colour distinct from fog, or lower fog opacity (`rgba(200,220,255,0.6)`). |
| **Performance drops dramatically** | Very high `exaggeration` + high zoom + many draped layers. | Keep exaggeration modest (≤2), limit overlay layers, and test on target devices. |

---

## 6. Optional – more “realistic” sky settings

MapLibre currently supports only the **default atmospheric sky** (no `sky-type` selector).  
You can still tweak the look:

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| `sky-color` | `#70a0ff` → `#a0d8ff` | Overall dome colour (day-time). |
| `horizon-color` | `#ffffff` → `#f0f0f0` | Brightness of the horizon line. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | `0.2` → `0.8` | How quickly the dome colour fades into the horizon. |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | `0.2` → `0.8` | How much the fog colour mixes into the horizon. |
| `fog-color` | Light blue-grey (`rgba(200,220,255,0.6)`) | Colour of the atmospheric haze. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | `0.2` → `0.7` | How far the fog reaches down the terrain. |

Experiment with these values until the sky matches the mood you want (clear day, hazy morning, etc.).

---

## 7. TL;DR – the minimal code you need

```js
// 1️⃣ Terrain (already on)
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

// 2️⃣ Pitch the camera (already on)
map.easeTo({ pitch: 70 });

// 3️⃣ Add the sky (the missing piece)
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#cce6ff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

That single `setSky` call (or the equivalent `sky` object in your style) turns the empty page background into a proper sky with atmospheric haze, completing the 3-D terrain experience. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe for putting a pre-rendered shaded-relief JPEG tile set under all of your vector data (including label layers) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣  What you need to know

| What you have | How MapLibre treats it |
|---------------|------------------------|
| **JPEG raster tiles** (e.g. `https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg`) | A **`raster` source** → a **`raster` layer**. No DEM, no hillshade, no `color-relief`. |
| **Vector data + label layers** | Usually added as `fill`, `line`, `symbol`, … layers. They sit **above** any raster layer that appears earlier in the style’s layer stack. |
| **Desired order** | 1️⃣ raster-relief → 2️⃣ all your vector layers (including the `symbol` label layer). |

> **Tip:** If you are using a style JSON that already contains a bunch of layers, you can insert the raster layer *right after* the background (or before the first vector layer) with `map.addLayer(..., beforeId)`. If you are building the style from scratch, just add the raster layer first and then the vector layers.

---

## 2️⃣  Minimal HTML / JS skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief basemap + vector data</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // a plain background so you don’t see a white flash while tiles load
      background: { type: 'background', paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' } },
      sources: {},   // we’ll fill this in below
      layers: []     // we’ll fill this in below
    },
    center: [0, 0],   // pick a sensible centre for your area
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the raster-relief source & layer
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // ---- 2a. raster source (JPEG tiles) ----
    map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: [
        // 👉  replace this URL template with yours
        'https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,          // most JPEG/PNG tiles are 256 px
      // optional: if your tiles are in WebP or another format, just change the extension
      // optional: set a maxzoom if you know the highest zoom you have
      // maxzoom: 14,
      // optional: set a minzoom if you have a lower-zoom limit
      // minzoom: 0,
      // optional: tell the browser to keep the tiles in memory a bit longer
      // promoteId: null
    });

    // ---- 2b. raster layer that draws the source ----
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'relief-basemap-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'relief-basemap',
      // paint properties are optional – they let you tweak opacity, fade, etc.
      paint: {
        // make the basemap a little translucent if you want the vector fill to “show through”
        // 'raster-opacity': 0.95,
        // control how quickly tiles fade in when they load (ms)
        // 'raster-fade-duration': 300
      }
    }, /* beforeId */ 'water'); // <-- put it before the first vector layer you have (example: 'water')
    // If you don’t know a layer id to insert before, just omit the second argument.
    // The raster layer will be added on top of the background but *below* any later layers you add.

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add your vector data (example: a GeoJSON source)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // You can add as many vector sources / layers as you like – they will automatically sit
    // above the raster basemap because they are added *after* it.
    map.addSource('countries', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datasets/geo-countries/master/data/countries.geojson'
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'countries-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'countries',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#4a90e2',
        'fill-opacity': 0.4
      }
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'countries-outline',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'countries',
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#003366',
        'line-width': 1
      }
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add a label layer (symbol) – will sit on top
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('city-points', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [
          { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-0.1276, 51.5074] }, properties: { name: 'London' } },
          { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [2.3522, 48.8566] }, properties: { name: 'Paris' } }
        ]
      }
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'city-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'city-points',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
        'text-size': 14,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
        'text-offset': [0, 0.6],
        'text-anchor': 'top'
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 2
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Initialize the map** | Starts with a *blank* style (`background` only) so we can inject our own sources/layers. |
| **Add a `raster` source** | Points MapLibre at your JPEG tile endpoint. The source type is **`raster`**, *not* `raster-dem`. |
| **Add a `raster` layer** | Draws the source. Because we add it **first**, it ends up under everything else. The optional `beforeId` argument (`'water'` in the example) shows how to insert it *right before* a known vector layer if you already have a style with many layers. |
| **Add vector layers** | Any `fill`, `line`, `symbol`, etc. you add **after** the raster layer will automatically be rendered on top. |
| **Add a label (`symbol`) layer** | Labels are just another vector layer; they sit on top of the fills/outlines because they are added last. |

---

## 3️⃣  Quick checklist for a production-ready implementation

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **Tile URL template** matches the exact pattern your tile server expects (`{z}/{x}/{y}`) and includes the correct file extension (`.jpg`, `.jpeg`, `.webp`, …). |
| **CORS** – Your tile server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) so the browser can fetch the tiles from a different origin. |
| **`tileSize`** – Most JPEG tiles are 256 px; if yours are 512 px set `tileSize: 512`. |
| **`maxzoom` / `minzoom`** – Declare the zoom range you actually have; this prevents MapLibre from requesting non-existent tiles. |
| **Opacity / Fade** – If the relief image is too “hard” against your vector fills, tweak `raster-opacity` (0 → 1) or add a subtle `raster-fade-duration`. |
| **Layer ordering** – Verify with the browser’s dev tools (`map.getStyle().layers`) that the raster layer’s `id` appears **before** any vector layer you want on top. |
| **Performance** – JPEG tiles are already compressed, but you can still enable `prefetchZoomDelta` or `tileCacheSize` on the map if you need smoother panning at high zoom. |
| **Responsive design** – The map container (`#map`) should be 100 % width/height or otherwise sized via CSS so the raster tiles fill the viewport correctly. |

---

## 4️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **The relief image never appears** | Wrong source type (`raster-dem`) or wrong URL template. | Use `type: 'raster'` and double-check the URL (open a tile directly in the browser). |
| **The raster shows but vector data is hidden underneath** | Raster layer added **after** the vector layers (i.e., on top). | Move the raster layer earlier (`map.moveLayer('relief-basemap-layer', 'water')` or re-add it with a `beforeId`). |
| **Tiles are blurry / pixelated** | `tileSize` mismatch (e.g., source tiles are 512 px but you told MapLibre they’re 256 px). | Set `tileSize` to the correct value. |
| **CORS error in console** | Tile server does not allow cross-origin requests. | Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) on the server, or serve tiles from the same origin. |
| **Map shows a white background while tiles load** | No background layer defined, causing a flash of white. | Keep the `background` layer (as in the skeleton) or set `background-color` to a neutral tone. |
| **Labels are hard to read over the relief** | Relief image is too dark or too bright. | Adjust `raster-opacity` (e.g., `0.85`) or add a subtle `raster-brightness-min/max` paint property. |

---

## 5️⃣  TL;DR – One-liner for a style JSON (if you already have a full style)

If you are editing a **style JSON** directly (e.g., you have a MapLibre style file you upload to a server), just insert the following two objects:

```json
{
  "sources": {
    "relief-basemap": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"],
      "tileSize": 256
    }
    // …your other sources here…
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "relief-basemap-layer",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "relief-basemap",
      "paint": {
        "raster-opacity": 1
      }
    },
    // 👉 Insert the raster layer **before** the first vector layer you want on top.
    // e.g. if your first vector layer is "landuse", place it right before that:
    //   "beforeId": "landuse"
    // (In a raw JSON file you just order the objects accordingly.)

    // …your existing vector layers (fills, lines, symbols) follow here…
  ]
}
```

Make sure the raster layer appears **earlier** in the `layers` array than any `fill`, `line`, or `symbol` layers you want on top.

---

### 🎉 Done!

You now have a **pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap** (JPEG tiles) sitting neatly under all of your vector data and labels. Feel free to tweak opacity, fade, or add additional raster layers (e.g., a satellite layer) using the same pattern—just keep the ordering in mind.

